# The twelve months round the year wheel, and a birthday's date.
lesson language-months v=1 format=teach grade=3 unit=8 subject=language levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Birthdays and months"
  goal "Say the months in [[language.name]] and give the date of a birthday."

  look {
    scene 17x17 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        yearwheel wheel seasons=true mark="May"
      }
    }
    say """
      The months: [[time.january]], [[time.february]], [[time.march]], [[time.april]], [[time.may]],
      [[time.june]], [[time.july]], [[time.august]], [[time.september]], [[time.october]],
      [[time.november]], [[time.december]]. To ask about a birthday, say
      [[phrase.when-is-your-birthday]] and answer [[phrase.it-is-the-fifth-of-april]], it is the
      fifth of April.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked language.months-name k=5
    }
    show language.months-name k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=7
    show language.months-meaning k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show language.months-write k=4 easy-k=2 hard-k=6
    show language.months-name k=5 easy-k=2 hard-k=8
    show language.months-match k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=3
    show language.months-write k=5 easy-k=4 hard-k=7
    show language.months-listen k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.months-say k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show language.months-write k=4
    }
  }
  language es {
    try stars=2 {
      show language.es-months-why k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    }
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show language.months-puzzle k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "[[time.january|cap]], [[time.february]], [[time.march]], [[time.april]], [[time.may]], [[time.june]], [[time.july]], [[time.august]], [[time.september]], [[time.october]], [[time.november]], [[time.december]]."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The wrong idea to watch for is June and July swapped, and English habits carried over into a
    date, which the two-star question asks about. The ringed month on the wheel is written in
    English, so on paper the question still reads. The listening question is a dictation: read the
    sentence aloud from this sheet, where it prints as Read aloud, or let the device play it. It is
    marked word by word, and a missing accent counts there, while the words typed on their own in
    this lesson are taken without their accents. If the child is stuck, go round the wheel from
    January saying each month and clapping. Off the page, write the birthdays of four people you
    know in the language. It meets England's key stage 2 languages programme (years 3 to 6,
    statutory from age 7): "write phrases from memory, and adapt these to create new sentences, to
    express ideas clearly"; Japan's grades 5 and 6 foreign languages: "Activities to listen to
    concrete information regarding familiar and simple matters in daily life such as dates, times
    and prices etc."; and CEFR A1, "Can understand and use familiar everyday expressions and very
    basic phrases". The phrases are in [[language.variety]].
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level [[time.march]] is worked first in the teacher's pen, the words to name are [[time.january]] and [[time.august]] and the words to write are [[time.april]] and [[time.july]], the plainer ones of the lesson, with the first set to match, the shortest sentence to hear and the first version of each star question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to name are [[time.june]] and [[time.september]] and the words to write are [[time.august]], [[time.october]] and [[time.july]], the ones most often mixed up, with the last set to match, the longest sentence to hear, a third word to write and the last version of each star question."
  }
}
